  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1144 PM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nCurrent trends in the models have now seen the expected upper-\nlevel trough and cold front to be more delayed with most agreeing\nto an arrival time closer to Saturday afternoon. Still it is more\ntypical for a front during this time of year to be more ahead of\nschedule than behind, so current confidence is more settled for\nthe late morning to early afternoon. Regardless, winds will be bit\nbreezy ahead of this front before quieting down later that\nevening. In terms of precipitation chances, latest deterministic\nmodels have continue to push some weak showers across the\nPanhandles with the front. However, ensembles are still not to\nthrilled on the idea with chances only around the far eastern\nPanhandles at 10 to 20 percent. Either way once the Panhandles are\nbehind the front, look for a brief period of dry and cooler air to\nfollow for Sunday. Moving into Monday and the holiday week, does\nsee our next system push across with latest ensembles giving us\naround 20 to 30 percent chances at precipitation at this time.\nWhat make this interesting is that current trends with model have\nbegun to favor overnight low reach near or below freeze, which\ncould result in our first opportunity at snow for the Northern\nPanhandles. However, given our current chances of actually seeing\nprecipitation, confidence is not too high just yet. Regardless\nthis active pattern look to have the opportunity to continue as\nmodels hint at yet another system for the holiday itself.\nOtherwise, temperatures will keep more of that late fall feel to\nthem with highs look to stay in the 40s through the week. &&",
